>>
endobj
103 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (Thus, he was primarily interested in human nature. Human nature can be analyzed in two ways: the compositive method and the resolutive method.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
104 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (The idea behind them is simple. A compositive analysis is when you begin from certain reasonable assumptions you have and then drawing out your theories from them. You are a thoroughgoing botanist and see a flower in an orange tree. With your knowledge about the nature of plants, you deduce that it will possibly grow into an orange. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
105 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (A resolutive analysis is when you actually look at the effects in the world \(or to simply observe\), and work backwards to find what may have caused them. For example, you may see an orange on the ground near an orange tree. You can reason backwards and argue that the orange may have been on the tree and once it ripened, it fell down. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 84 0 R
/Pg 12 0 R
/A [218 0 R 0]
/K [4]
>>
